WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:15.599
Thank you very much.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:22.960
I should say that after our next speaker
we're delighted to have Yale's political science

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.399
eminence,

00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:28.409
Professor Doug Ray, author of "City", a remarkable
book that deals with the

00:00:28.409 --> 00:00:30.480
origins of some of these problems

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.629
in our very own city

00:00:31.629 --> 00:00:33.480
who will

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.910
moderate the questions afterwards. But first

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:42.910
Tom Vanderbilt.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.430
Okay, great, can everyone hear me okay?

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:50.890
Great to see such a

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.870
big turnout here.

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.510
I was just at a conference in Austin for the Texas
Department of Transportation.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.880
One of the commissioners there was decrying

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.310
what he thought the view of transportation
as a central importance to our lives.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:04.879
The view of transportation

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.549
to the average person

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:09.180
as he put it in I thought a very Texas way, was about

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:16.180
as sexy as putting socks on a rooster.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:20.630
So to use Don's analogy of dogs and cats,

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.680
I tend to think I was sort of a pidgin,

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.040
kind of flitting about the streets, picking up
what I could hear and there from all sorts of

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:27.710
different aspects

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.840
of traffic, and this is

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.670
the book I've written. Don is one of the many people
in it, whose work I was really trying to

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.049
bring to this wider public. And I'm a

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:39.750
journalist by training, no background
in transportation planning or

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.810
anything like that. Just the presence of
this here is making me very

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.080
nervous.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.660
These are the sorts of things I would typically skip in

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.360
a lot of transportation papers and get to the
conclusion to find out

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.600
the truth.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.180
So anyways, this is the book I've written. It's
been published in a number of different countries,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.160
which is

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.690
interesting.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:09.840
And this is a fascinating process,
not without a few moments lost in translation.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.999
If you notice in the German edition down here in the
lower left hand corner,

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.999
they went with the title "Auto" instead of "Traffic",
and I

00:02:15.999 --> 00:02:19.999
called up the publisher. I said this is a little different,

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:21.129
I'm curious about this. It's not really a book about the car,
it's more about traffic, and they said, well, 

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:25.369
Tom, in German the word "traffic" is actually
synonymous with the word for intercourse.

00:02:26.699 --> 00:02:29.829
And I spent a few minutes on the phone trying to explain
why this actually wouldn't be a bad thing to

00:02:29.829 --> 00:02:30.999
confuse the mind of

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:32.769
a consumer,

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:33.500
but

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.260
he didn't buy that.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.839
I just wanted to begin...

00:02:37.839 --> 00:02:40.790
So I live in Brooklyn. I have a particularly unique

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:44.689
relationship to parking. I do own a car, which is a very
strange thing, and we have a very

00:02:44.689 --> 00:02:47.699
interesting form of what's called transportation
demand management in New York City, which

00:02:47.699 --> 00:02:48.439
is that you

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:52.569
basically don't leave your space, you don't
ever drive because of fear

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:55.180
not only losing a great space you have, but
of encountering

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.999
sort of angry natives when you return and
do something wrong.

00:02:58.999 --> 00:03:03.099
But even within New York-- a minority
of New Yorkers actually own cars--

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:04.880
but even within New York there's interesting

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.789
variations between neighborhoods. I know
there's a Jackson Heights

00:03:07.789 --> 00:03:10.830
resident--former Jackson Heights resident-- here somewhere,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.789
and an interesting study looked at two neighborhoods
in Brooklyn, which is where I live, and

00:03:14.789 --> 00:03:17.959
you know if you're transportation planner
sort of looking at this, you might

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:22.099
go up and down the line here and think that well
Park Slope here is a wealthier

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:25.139
part of town. There's a few more cars there.

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:28.419
Certainly they must sending more vehicles
the central business district.

00:03:28.419 --> 00:03:30.760
In fact it was actually Jackson Heights, and something

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.519
like forty percent more people were actually driving
into the central business district, and

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:35.989
the reason was that

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:38.060
Jackson Heights people tend to have

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.809
little parking spaces protected. They have curb
cuts, they have garages,

00:03:41.809 --> 00:03:43.479
so this is something that

00:03:43.479 --> 00:03:47.849
the mere presence of this protected parking
spot emboldened them to drive and

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:49.599
change their behavior in a way that...

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:53.430
…It's sort of Economics 101. Incentives matter.
Whereas I

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.349
am kind of afraid to drive anywhere.

00:03:56.349 --> 00:04:00.399
This is sort of a 1970 image,
sort of the high point of getting

00:04:00.399 --> 00:04:02.509
over the city to the automobile.

00:04:02.509 --> 00:04:06.159
I'm a child of the early 1970's, and
it's been interesting to sort of note the

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:09.809
sort of unsustainable path we've been on. We talk about
the American

00:04:09.809 --> 00:04:12.909
love affair with the automobile, 

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:15.940
and some people joke that it's long ago
turned into a marriage, but

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:20.109
it's interesting for me to track how much
this has changed even in the past two decades since

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:22.319
I've been around.

00:04:22.319 --> 00:04:26.400
Another way to look at parking here,
this is Brian Pijanowski, a geographer

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.659
at Purdue who

00:04:27.659 --> 00:04:32.429
did a sort of heat map here of all the available
parking spaces in Tippecanoe county,

00:04:32.429 --> 00:04:34.140
Indiana. These are just the municipal

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.819
sort of parking lot. This isn't residential stuff.

00:04:36.819 --> 00:04:41.299
He found there are basically three times as many
parking spaces as there are actual residents in the

00:04:41.299 --> 00:04:42.119
town,

00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:43.270
that

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.259
parking lots occupied twenty percent more space than the

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:48.719
businesses they served.

00:04:48.719 --> 00:04:51.159
That if you were to extend this relationship to the

00:04:51.159 --> 00:04:52.700
country, to America,

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.599
Connecticut and Massachusetts would have to be turned into
entirely parking lots,

00:04:56.599 --> 00:05:00.870
and that basically that if everyone in this county got
up and drove around at the same time,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.310
there'd be something on the order of 250,000

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.479
parking spaces still available, just sort of
sitting there.

00:05:06.479 --> 00:05:08.599
So massive sort of overabundance of

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:13.280
parking and I should say road space.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.870
The Rand Corporation has estimated that ninety percent
of American roads are not congested ninety

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.419
percent of time.

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:22.379
But, you know, this is another thing that's
changed remarkably. In 1960, this

00:05:22.379 --> 00:05:26.499
was almost unheard of, a three-car garage. Now
one in five new homes

00:05:26.499 --> 00:05:28.359
has one of these.

00:05:28.359 --> 00:05:33.009
And what's interesting is that this sort of
relationship began to change even as American

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:37.240
households were getting smaller, and arguably
houses for the many smaller. But it

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:42.130
sort of speaks to the idea that we hear them talk
about the population

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.030
increase contributing to congestion, and in 
some cases it does, but

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:47.870
what's really changed over the past few decades
is this

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:50.659
incredible increase in vehicle miles traveled, which is
VMT of course.

00:05:50.659 --> 00:05:54.010
From 1968 to 2000,

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:58.459
the population grew about forty percent, the number
the number of vehicle miles travelled

00:05:58.459 --> 00:06:00.699
increased 160%, so

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:04.840
I sort of, and most of this I should say
was what's called discretionary driving,

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:05.830
not work-related

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.260
commuting,

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.699
this driving, so

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:12.650
I tend to blame the rise of organized
youth soccer,

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.239
for the sort of

00:06:14.239 --> 00:06:16.800
fast increase that was going on here. But

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.759
there's some other things kind of rising at a
similar rate

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:21.330
during that period.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.970
And we lay a lot of blame
at the foot of sort of corn syrup and things

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:27.080
like that, but

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:28.740
clearly this

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.460
tracks the VMT increase pretty well.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.069
Sheldon Jacobson has pointed out that as
Americans have gotten heavier, this is of course

00:06:35.069 --> 00:06:37.319
in a sort of perverse feedback loop,

00:06:37.319 --> 00:06:40.059
our fuel economy has declined because it

00:06:40.059 --> 00:06:41.919
requires more energy to move

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:45.259
these heavier American around, so it's sort of
a negative feedback loop.

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:47.789
Of course we have ways of counteracting

00:06:47.789 --> 00:06:51.089
this obesity thing. We

00:06:51.089 --> 00:06:55.360
drive to the gym, and this is an actual...
it's in San Diego…it's an

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:57.800
actual gym. Some people think this photo
was doctored, but I 

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.150
borrowed this from Dan Burden, this

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.389
great image.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:06.589
Now this is--talk about being a child of the 70's--this is an iconic house, the Brady Bunch house.

00:07:06.589 --> 00:07:07.809
And we think about

00:07:07.809 --> 00:07:10.879
suburbia, obviously people do more driving,
but even again

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:14.219
in these past few decades as a measure 
of how things have changed.

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:17.979
The Brady Bunch house on the walk
score web site interestingly

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:19.809
scores an 82, which is

00:07:19.809 --> 00:07:21.039
not the 

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:23.419
98 percent that my Brooklyn neighborhood does,
but still

00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:25.460
pretty decent compared to

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:30.369
what's come along since then, which is places like Henderson,
Nevada, one of the epicenters of the subprime

00:07:30.369 --> 00:07:33.809
crisis, were you're getting walk scores in the neighborhood
of about 50.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.429
Now just another thing that has changed
in that time.

00:07:40.429 --> 00:07:43.919
First McDonald's drive thru came out in 1975.
I always thought this happened much

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:44.679
earlier, but

00:07:44.679 --> 00:07:49.330
McDonald's now does a majority of their business
through the drive thru, so McDonald's is sort of a nice little

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:54.559
icon of car dependency. McDonald's has 
become a car independent country.

00:07:54.559 --> 00:07:58.369
So these demographic things are interesting, but wasn't
really what motivated me to write

00:07:58.369 --> 00:08:00.419
the book. What really motivated me was

00:08:00.419 --> 00:08:02.300
just some sort of

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.029
questions I had as a long-time driver, participant in

00:08:05.029 --> 00:08:08.629
this environment that I think is so ubiquitous
and yet overlook, and so

00:08:08.629 --> 00:08:10.849
I was trying to sort of understand some of the

00:08:10.849 --> 00:08:14.429
things about the behavior of other drivers, about
the way traffic worked,

00:08:14.429 --> 00:08:17.270
sort of understand this

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.179
environment. And so I had

00:08:19.179 --> 00:08:22.339
simple questions in mind that I wanted to ask.
I want to show you a clip

00:08:22.339 --> 00:08:24.789
from a film that I think demonstrates one of these

00:08:24.789 --> 00:08:27.089
traffic phenomena quite well.

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:29.949
We can just go through this, this is Office Space.

00:08:29.949 --> 00:08:36.949
The phenomenon I'm talking about

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.770
is the sensation you may have in traffic when the other
lane always moves faster. Okay so now this is a really, a real
phenomenon that i think we've all experienced,

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.400
and what's interesting though is that some
Canadian researchers actually

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.850
identified what was going on here. It's an interesting
cognitive illusion because about

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:56.370
ninety percent of our time spent driving
we're looking forward, which sort of makes

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:56.860
sense.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:00.190
There's something that happens by which we actually
spend more time watching other vehicles

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:03.490
physically pass us than we see ourselves passing
other vehicles.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:07.840
This illusion is made worse by the closer
we drive to the car in front of us

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.550
and the more glances we make in the neighboring lane.
It increases the sense of this

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:15.970
kind of illusion. So we always sort of feel that we're coming
out on the losing end in a traffic situation.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:18.600
This I think plays into what Daniel Kahneman has called

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.550
loss aversion, by which the human brain actually
registers losses much more powerfully than it does gains.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.560
And this is a case,

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.790
if you talk to many traffic engineers, they would say well
in many cases it would be better if no one actually

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:29.939
changed lanes,

00:10:29.939 --> 00:10:34.259
what seems best for the individual user, user
optimality, actually makes the system work.

00:10:34.259 --> 00:10:36.870
This guy's probalby changing lanes because someone
did what he did

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.310
about a mile ahead and there's sort of a

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.910
butterfly effect that can happen that is
almost a version of chaos theory.

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:45.160
Now another question

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:46.080
I had was

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.030
this idea of congestion, was it always

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:53.410
an instance of there not being enough capacity,
was there always a bottleneck, was it a result of an

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:56.030
accident on-ramp, adding new vehicles to a system.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:58.230
Some Japanese physicists did a

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:02.970
fascinating experiment. They got a closed track,
and asked a group of drivers to

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.020
maintain a steady speed and following distance

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:07.740
and keep this up for as long as they could,

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:11.350
and you'll see that because humans are not robotic
devices that

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:16.700
over time a bit of sort of noise begins
to enter the system, people begin to drive

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:21.940
closer, they fail to react in time, they overreact,
they brake harder than they should...

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.970
And out of this sort of perfect closed system,

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:27.980
stop and go congestion begins to emerge

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:29.020
 

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.410
just the sheer actions of drivers, and

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:35.190
there's been some interesting modeling done looking
at people on cellphones because you have a delayed

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:35.740
reaction.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:37.740
People on cellphones actually

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.720
exacerbating the situation, making it worse.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.820
Now there's a solution that's out there, which I rode in
New York about a year ago. This

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:45.570
is a

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:50.160
vehicle called Junior, which is the Stanford
University entry in the DARPA Urban

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:51.150
Challenge.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.220
A fully automated Volkswagen Passat. 

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.590
I rode for him for a few blocks. Junior can

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:58.890
sort of see everything around with radar 
and a variety of censors.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:02.380
Unlike many New York City drivers, he actually
has full compliance with

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:05.380
various traffic signals, stop signs and

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:07.790
after few blocks you sort of get used to the idea that

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:09.650
you're being piloted around by this

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:13.910
automatic vehicle. Of course, Junior would have
no trouble maintaining this steady speed and following

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:15.140
distance, so this is one

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:17.560
version of the future, which is coming.

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:22.010
Now another idea of the way we contribute
to our own traffic problems, of course the famous one is

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:22.990
rubbernecking.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.060
Some Dutch researchers

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:26.680
sent me this a while ago, and they

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:28.530
did interesting sort of...

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.940
…and waited for a crash to happen

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:32.630
on a Dutch highway,

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.689
and this was a van rollover on top here,

00:12:35.689 --> 00:12:38.040
luckily not fatal, but what you see is

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:39.740
obviously you see there's a

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:42.900
capacity drop in the top section here. People
are having to move over,

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.540
do some merging activity,

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:47.960
there's about a twelve percent reduction,
I think they estimated.

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:51.770
But what's interesting--so there's a physical bottleneck--
but if you look at the very wide median strip

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:53.070
across there,

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:56.470
vehicles are actually slowing exactly where the van is,
there's sort of a mental bottleneck

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:57.590
that has emerged.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:03.450
They also modeled that the drivers were merging
from this queue slower than they would in

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:04.960
normal stop and go congestion.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:07.110
So clearly that accident was having an effect on them.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:10.300
They're sort of distracted, perhaps pausing to contemplate

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:12.359
their own mortality.

00:13:12.359 --> 00:13:15.610
This is something we all sort of know anecdotally
happens, but it's nice to see

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:19.660
the actual of data out there and have this stuff modeled.

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.490
So, the reviewer for the New York Times

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:25.580
suggested that my book should be called simply "Idiots",

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:29.220
and this was one question I was tasked with,
which was why people seemed to

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:30.120
do these

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:34.330
dumb things on the road as a California
highway patrol officer put it to me, why we seem

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:37.450
to act on the road as if we were suffering 
from a cranial-rectal inversion.

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:39.910
Part of the problem I think is we

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.460
often underestimate the sheer complexity of driving.
It's been estimated for example that every two

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:47.180
miles the average driver makes 400 observations,
40 decisions,

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.130
and one mistake.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:50.649
But the idea, and I was really

00:13:50.649 --> 00:13:54.850
trying to get to this idea that Jacques Tati
got to in his film "Traffic", which I would recommend

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:55.990
if haven't seen it,

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:58.880
which is the idea that we become different
people when we enter

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:01.030
an automobile.

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:04.680
Clearly, and for the traffic engineers in the
house tonight,

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:07.990
managing traffic and mobility is sort of a long-standing
problem. This is

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:10.450
an image that comes from the ruins of Pompeii.

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:13.990
You see the chariot ruts still extant in the street.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:17.980
They've put these sort of walkways across
there

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:21.649
so pedestrians can cross without being
drenched by mud.

00:14:21.649 --> 00:14:23.420
And you see it's lined up perfectly

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:26.580
so that the chariots can cross through these crossings.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.370
It's kind of an interesting echo of today's 
speed cushions,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.890
traffic calming devices, which have nice cuts so

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:33.510
emergency vehicles can drive over them

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:35.930
faster than normal cars.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.759
Pompeii even had some early bollards,

00:14:38.759 --> 00:14:43.240
devices to block vehicles from using
certain streets.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:47.540
And this idea of managing different modes
of mobility and tension on the road,

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:49.670
road rage, the modern

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:52.530
phrase. Actually it's not modern at all. 

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.100
This is a painting from the nineteenth century.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.340
Managing these various loads has been a longstanding
problem, and

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:02.880
just when it seemed 

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.230
as if things could not get more complicated on the
road along came this new 

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:07.760
form of personal mobility,

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.600
the first real innovation since Caesar's Rome,

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.170
which upset this fragile balance.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:15.530
I'm talking here about the bicycle of course. This

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:19.100
incredibly controversial device in the late nineteenth
century. The New York Times called them

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:22.600
rubber-shod missiles of the highway.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:26.180
Horse carriages didn't want them in the street, 
pedestrians didn't want them on the sidewalk. Where

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:27.450
do you put this thing?

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.480
Strange new etiquette questions: should men yield to women?

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:35.860
So this is an ancient sort of problem, and

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:38.880
it brings up something that's been termed a
modal bias, which

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.490
when you enter a car, this idea that you become

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:44.700
sort of transformed to another sort of person.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.400
This comes from the Motor Mania, which was a 
Walt Disney short from the 1950s.

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:50.770
I think it gets to this wonderfully. It starts, and Goofy,

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:53.460
the dog, begins life as Mr. Walker,

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:54.260
a very sort of

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:56.120
innocent person, wouldn't hurt anyone,

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:59.100
becomes a homicidal maniac the second he enters

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:03.290
an automobile. 

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:19.880
So I think humans have trouble often looking outside of ourselves,
and there's something ecologists talk about, which is called

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:22.060
the fundamental attribution error,

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:27.010
in which we we basically attribute the actions
of others to something about their nature or their

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:29.550
character, and when we're asked to explain
our own actions,

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:32.080
we attribute it to situational factors.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:36.530
You fell; I was pushed. THat's a nice phrase to sort of
summarize this. I think we see this

00:16:36.530 --> 00:16:38.920
in driving a lot in part because of stress,

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:42.670
in part because we're often acting on such imperfect
information.

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:43.740
We make

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:46.610
judgments based on the slightest bit of extra
information such as

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:49.180
a person's license plate.

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:52.950
And this is one of the iron laws of driving in America,
the worst drivers are always in the neighboring

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:53.470
state.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:58.630
And sometimes we get this information quite
wrong in interesting ways. One of my favorite studies

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:02.190
looked at…students looked at two clips

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.280
of a car being driven, a BMW and

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:05.970
a sort of smaller VW.

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:10.270
They were asked a week later to estimate the
speed at which each vehicle was traveling at.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:14.620
A firm majority of subjects said the BMW must
have been driven faster.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:18.620
In fact they were driven at exactly the same speed. 
So we have these sort of mental models of what's happening

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:19.510
out there

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:20.949
that often don't

00:17:20.949 --> 00:17:24.959
correlate to reality.

00:17:24.959 --> 00:17:29.340
Now one classic probelm with traffic and
driver behavior is anonymity, so

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:32.690
I think I'll just talk a little about
the online disinhibition effect

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:35.610
on which we sort of sign on to an anonymous

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.870
chat room, we're protected behind a username that
is not our own.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:39.909
We're free to

00:17:39.909 --> 00:17:43.010
sort of act how we want to act without any repercussion.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:47.950
I think you can think of traffic this way
with millions of sort of anonymous users interacting

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:48.779
daily,

00:17:48.779 --> 00:17:51.450
protected by our license plates.

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:58.080
We do not see the same people
twice in traffic. It's sort of an

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.550
obvious thing, but it brings up some interesting

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:04.320
experimental games that have been done in economics, 
looking at trying to get people to cooperate when you have

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:05.870
to do things like contribute

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:07.570
money to a common pool,

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:09.840
the cooperation rate seems to soar

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.990
when people have the ability to punish people
in later rounds.

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:14.960
In traffic we kind of lack this

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:18.310
accountability, the ability to punish
other people. And of course there are some web sites

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:19.590
that have come up

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:23.590
where you can, sort of, you know, make comments about other
drivers, but not a lot of people sort of look at

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:24.890
these systems, which

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:28.270
makes it tricky.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:33.559
A somewhat related problem is overconfidence. 
Drivers are famously overconfident, and

00:18:33.559 --> 00:18:37.909
one place overconfidence flourishes in the lack of
effusive feedback. Now airline pilots

00:18:37.909 --> 00:18:39.140
are trained

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:42.350
rigorously in the importance of accepting feedback
from the co-pilots.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:46.550
Drivers are less receptive to this sort of 
feedback from their passengers

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:49.460
to put it politely, but passengers are literally

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:51.810
life-saving devices. Studies have been done.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:55.190
Solo drivers are involved in crashes at
a higher rate than people with passengers.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:57.200
One reason passengers are good for us

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:00.950
is they provide something we're often missing in
driving, and that's feedback.

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:03.350
And interesting work

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:07.380
done neurologically looking at the
mind of a driver while they're in a

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:09.140
simulated driving environment versus

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:14.630
a passenger. Entirely different neural networks being
activated. They are in essence different people.

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:17.450
On the notion of feedback, I for the last few months
had a

00:19:17.450 --> 00:19:21.600
small device plugged into the on board
diagnostic port in my car,

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.380
which measures, which is that kind of thing

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:26.490
mechanics look at when your check engine light goes on, and 

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:30.559
it actually has a lot of interesting information that
courses there that's pretty much ignored.

00:19:30.559 --> 00:19:33.700
And with an accelerometer and a GPS device, this little

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:37.380
device I have plugged in there tracks my driving,
tells me all sorts of things about it,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.740
gives me a report card at the end of the

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:41.420
month. Things like hard braking,

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:43.150
jack rabbit starts, even

00:19:43.150 --> 00:19:46.290
idling, and idling is one of these, you know,
the sort of American idle,

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.480
we consume

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:50.169
the amount of energy that Costa Rica

00:19:50.169 --> 00:19:54.710
expends in a year just by our idling.

00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:57.180
So often people aren't aware of this
behavior, and

00:19:57.180 --> 00:20:00.620
feedback is a kind of mechanism to make them aware.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:01.809
I've sort of

00:20:01.809 --> 00:20:03.580
even driving up here today,

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:05.670
the CEO of this company,

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:06.790
whose name is David,

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:07.900
I've started to

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:12.150
call the device David. that's sitting in my
car. I sort of feel like he's watching over me

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:13.620
as I'm driving.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:17.600
Someone cuts in front of me, I have to brake
harder than I want to,

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:20.860
I sort of think David's going to think I was 
driving recklessly, but it was really that person's fault.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:26.550
So, one problem with feedback, though, is delivering it effectively,
and a guy named Clifford Nass at Stanford has done some

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:27.510
interesting

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:32.130
research for BMW and found out when male drivers were given
warnings

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:35.549
by a female voice in their car that their driving
needed to be improved,

00:20:35.549 --> 00:20:42.549
their driving actually deteriorated.

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:45.480
Another problem with traffic is that we

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:49.320
lack traditional signals for human communication,
which

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:50.510
Americans particularly

00:20:50.510 --> 00:20:53.550
are constantly trying to say things to one another, but

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:57.210
I think that traffic is sort of a living laboratory of
human interaction. I took this image

00:20:57.210 --> 00:20:57.900
in Beijing, a place where

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:01.440
one form of transportation is yielding

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:03.240
to another quite literally,

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:08.440
but it's a place thriving with these sort of subtle displays
of implied power. It's basically a giant applied psychology

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:09.050
laboratory.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:10.510
And this is a…

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:11.930
you can see this

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:15.690
at work in parking lots appropriately. One
of my favorite studies looked at people

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:16.040
who were

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:18.140
waiting to vacate their parking space.

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:20.420
They found that when this was actually measured,

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:25.140
when another driver was waiting for that driver to leave
the parking space, the person took longer

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:27.560
to leave that space than when no one was
waiting.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:29.170
Even though on surveys they said they

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:31.840
wouldn't do this. Another great place to

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.470
see this behavior at work

00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:35.360
is at

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:36.190
intersections,

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:39.270
traffic lights, and psychologists will do an
interesting experiment where they'll

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.850
stop a car at a red light,

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:42.390
wait till the light turns green,

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:44.010
and then not move that vehicle.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.250
And then measure who's sort of behind there,

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:47.500
how quickly they honk,

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:50.830
how many times they honk, how long each honk is

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:52.530
because honking, you know, is sort of a subtle

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:54.290
language going on there.

00:21:54.290 --> 00:21:57.700
They find that the patterns are decidedly not random.

00:21:57.700 --> 00:21:59.850
Men honk more quickly than women,

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:01.730
people in expensive cars

00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:05.110
are quicker to the horn than people driving
more modest vehicles.

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:09.030
People in convertibles with the top
down were less likely to honk than others.

00:22:09.030 --> 00:22:12.270
When the driver appeared to be
from a different country,

00:22:12.270 --> 00:22:16.090
and when the driver had appeared to be on a cellphone,
this generated a faster honk

00:22:16.090 --> 00:22:17.520
than in

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:20.720
other conditions.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:23.840
So there's all sorts of these, you know,

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:27.820
almost subconscious behaviors going on out there as
we move around, and we don't necessarily

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:29.570
think about it, which is one of the things that

00:22:29.570 --> 00:22:31.840
fascinates me about the traffic environment,
that is

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:35.770
a guy named Ian Walker who is also in the book, a traffic
psychologist from England did an

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:38.360
interesting set of experiments, rode around on
his bike,

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:39.720
hooked up a censor,

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:42.159
which measured the distance of passing cars

00:22:42.159 --> 00:22:43.830
to his bicycle, and he

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:46.950
rode in a variety of different conditions.

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:50.440
He put a helmet on, he did not put a helmet on, and he
wore what

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:52.750
he deemed to be a feminine wig. And he found that

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.499
people's passing distances

00:22:54.499 --> 00:22:57.270
varied with each conditions, when he had the helmet
on,

00:22:57.270 --> 00:22:59.520
drivers passed more closely to him

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:01.330
than when he was not wearing the helmet, and

00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:03.560
when he had the feminine wig,

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.820
drivers actually gave him more space. This might have been

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:11.900
actually confusion that he was actually Weird
Al Yankovic.

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:15.440
Now, another thing that Ian has talked
a lot about is the

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:18.470
importance of eye contact in the traffic environment,
something that we don't necessarily

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:20.289
think about that much, but

00:23:20.289 --> 00:23:22.050
something that is generally missing.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:24.039
If you look in the image on the left,

00:23:24.039 --> 00:23:26.220
I'm not sure if this works way in the back there.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:29.110
Something probably looks a little bit off about 
this image.

00:23:29.110 --> 00:23:33.310
What's happening is that Ian's, one of his eyes
is slightly shifted by a few pixels out of a

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:34.690
field of hundreds,

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:35.750
and we're incredibly sensitive to this,

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:37.810
we can sort of pick it out

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:39.750
from across the room. And

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:42.500
psychologists have even done eye-tracking experiments

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:44.330
where they look at where people are looking

00:23:44.330 --> 00:23:46.480
when they first look at an image of a car,

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:50.740
and they find that their eyes are drawn
to the headlights which resemble eyes.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:53.240
And there's a good reason we're so sensitive to this
eye contact

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:56.430
because it's an important tool in gaining social
cooperation.

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.110
And a lot of studies have shown this. One of
my favorite

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:01.230
takes place in a university break room.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:02.740
There was an

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:04.650
honor system for paying for coffee.

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:06.140
Take your coffee, put in

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:07.410
the requested amount.

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:11.670
And above the coffee machine they put on alternating weeks
an image of human eyes

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:13.590
or an image of flowers.

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:17.360
And you see that on the weeks the human eyes were there,
the donations actually soared,

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:20.800
and obviously they don't need to be real human
eyes.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.510
Week number one interestingly had sort of the highest
compliance rate

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:26.820
with the sort of

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:30.410
scary set of eyes.

00:24:30.410 --> 00:24:34.110
Will this work in traffic? This is
sort of an interesting question.

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:37.830
This is from an artistic traffic calming project
in St. Paul, Minnesota, which has actually been

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:39.970
pretty successful, and they just renewed it.

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:47.470
So, one of the themes in the book
is how people sort of react to the road environment

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:50.920
as a piece of design, and how this has sort of been
neglected I think by

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:53.770
traffic engineers over the last few decades
but is becoming to be

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:56.000
viewed with more importance. And

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.779
instead of simply erecting more signs,
beginning to think about

00:24:58.779 --> 00:25:02.380
other ways we can inform drivers about how to behave
in certain environments.

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:03.910
And when I began this

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:08.020
book, this was something I grew up with and 
still see all over, and I thought

00:25:08.020 --> 00:25:11.720
you know, isn't that nice, people
sort of care about our children. They're

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:14.030
out there sort of trying to protect them.

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:17.380
It turns out that these signs have been shown
to do absolutely nothing at all in terms

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:20.180
of speed reduction or improve the safety

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:21.970
children. They're sort of been banned from

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:24.100
the official MUTCD

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:27.890
because essentially they don't work. What usually
happens is that people are complaining about people

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:28.370
driving fast

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:29.750
down their street.

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:31.680
They call up the local engineers who

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:35.450
put up one of these as sort of a parental pacification
device and

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:37.200
walk away.

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:41.790
Speed management is sort of a huge issue

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:43.440
in America where

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:46.410
New York City we're about to try to get a 
twenty mile an hour

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:51.300
speed limit campaign going. Twenty's plenty
is the idea here, but

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:54.610
a lot of different approaches here to managing
speed.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:58.810
Interesting one here in Japan, Melody Road.
It features feedback, that if you

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:02.750
drive over at the proper speed, it plays a song.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:04.670
If you don't drive over, it doesn't.

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:09.560
Here's another approach in Durham, North Carolina.

00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:13.090
Now one reason, you know, to think about one reason 
that drivers might be speeding

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:15.490
is that there's a variety of sort of

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:18.120
visual illusions that happen when we're in the car.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.400
This is one of my favorite illusions.

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:24.640
It's essentially called treadmill effect. And the idea
here is to stare at the x in the center as 

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:26.750
carefully as you can, as closely as you can

00:26:26.750 --> 00:26:29.929
and if it works, you should see the Buddha

00:26:29.929 --> 00:26:35.730
sort of bloom out at you. Is that working for everyone?

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.409
And you know what's going on here is
that

00:26:37.409 --> 00:26:40.879
similar to a treadmill in a gym, when you
run on it for a while, then you stop, and the room

00:26:40.879 --> 00:26:42.350
seems to surge backwards.

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:45.649
When you drive at a higher speed, at a certain speed
for a good amount of time

00:26:45.649 --> 00:26:47.440
and then attempt to slow down,

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:49.350
the longer you've been driving at that higher speed,

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:53.150
the longer it actually takes you to slow down, for your
brain to sort of mentally catch up,

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:55.549
the neurons that track forward and backward motion

00:26:55.549 --> 00:26:56.899
are getting confused.

00:26:56.899 --> 00:26:59.180
It's sort of a long story, but

00:26:59.180 --> 00:27:03.299
engineers, you know, are forced
to put these sort of dynamic signs that actually

00:27:03.299 --> 00:27:05.470
remind drivers of how fast they're going as

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:08.960
they're entering an environment.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:12.030
Another problem though is that a lot of people
are living on streets like this, and no matter

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:13.340
what signs

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:15.980
an engineer is going to put up on a street
like this,

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:20.070
this street is sending a variety of overt, 
not-so-subtle messages that this is an environment

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:20.970
for cars.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:24.480
I don't know why there are traffic lights on
on this first bit of street.

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:27.810
One of my favorite phrases, a Canadian engineer
named Ezra Hauer said

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:30.159
drivers adapt to the road they see,

00:27:30.159 --> 00:27:33.180
and this road is clearly sending a very
strong

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:33.860
message about how to

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:36.440
behave. It reminds me of a

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:39.710
concept that Cornell University researcher,
Brian Wansink

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:41.870
calls portions distortion.

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:45.100
He's done studies that show people consume
measurably more food

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:46.900
when it's served to them in a larger

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.540
package,

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:52.690
more so than they actually even want. He's done experiments
with stale popcorn in movie theaters.

00:27:52.690 --> 00:27:55.860
People consume more stale popcorn when they 
eat it out of a larger bucket.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:57.309
So I think

00:27:57.309 --> 00:28:01.650
a lot of American roads suffer from a version of
this portion distortion. They're over-engineered

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:02.850
for safety.

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:04.180
People drive fast, and then

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:05.530
they're confused--

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:10.440
we're surprised when people consume all the extra
safety that's been built in.

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:13.360
This is another

00:28:13.360 --> 00:28:16.260
image from a small town in Texas. This
is something that

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.190
has been happening around the country:

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:19.419
street trees,

00:28:19.419 --> 00:28:21.500
often like this, are sort of torn out.

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:25.730
Is the tree a hazard, or is the
tree in fact a safety device?

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:28.140
I can sort of guarantee the moment you take that tree out,

00:28:28.140 --> 00:28:31.000
speeds are going to increase, which is probably going to
pose a greater

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.370
risk to any pedestrians or cyclists

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:36.420
on that street. The tree is sort of an obvious

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.200
hazard, but because of various legal liability issues

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:40.809
we've been tearing these things out,

00:28:40.809 --> 00:28:43.110
I think to our detriment throughout the country.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:45.840
I could go on about the problems of signs, 

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:47.090
over signage. Sometimes we don't

00:28:47.090 --> 00:28:49.179
actually, aren't able to see them,

00:28:49.179 --> 00:28:51.429
sometimes they sort of state

00:28:51.429 --> 00:28:52.909
the sort of obvious,

00:28:52.909 --> 00:28:56.400
sometimes they send... sometimes we selectively
obey signs

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:59.710
based on what we want to do,

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:01.630
and sometimes we simply don't

00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:03.560
understand what they're trying to tell us.

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:08.059
I see this one in London all the time, and
every time I come across it I feel like I'm having

00:29:08.059 --> 00:29:10.610
a mid-life crisis.

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:13.270
This should be on a cubicle wall or something, giving me,
you know,

00:29:13.270 --> 00:29:20.270
positive life direction, you know.

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:23.300
One of my favorite studies I've looked at

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:24.720
had people try to--

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:27.870
the point here is that people often don't
understand what signs are telling them,

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:29.720
but one of my favorite studies had people look at

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:31.730
a watch for falling rocks sign.

00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:35.100
Half the drivers said that they would look for rocks 
falling in the moment and speed up,

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:38.250
have said they would slow down and look for rocks
already on the road, so...

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:45.250
I sometimes wonder if engineers really just need to
sort of spell out everything.

00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:50.550
Now I'll conclude with perhaps one of the most absurd

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:53.510
warning sign cases. This involved

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:57.720
moose advisories in Newfoundland, Canada.
There was a foggy stretch of road that was home to not

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:59.990
only many car-moose collisions but

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:04.270
also many cars and cars stopping to take pictures of moose.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:05.179
So signs...

00:30:05.179 --> 00:30:08.760
so engineers figured so how can we combat this problem. They

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:11.830
erected full-size reflective silhouettes of moose,

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:16.040
thinking this with would help the situation.

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:18.380
Unfortunately, tourists found these pretty interesting, too,

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:22.690
and as they stopped to take pictures or the moose signs,
the moose signs themselves

00:30:22.690 --> 00:30:24.350
became crash hot spots.

00:30:24.350 --> 00:30:25.840
The next logical step,

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:30.120
and I wish this were a joke, it actually isn't,
was to create new signs that read caution moose

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:33.330
signs ahead.

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:38.300
So, just take a look at the signs you see around you
and wonder you know who's looking at them

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:40.440
and what are they actually doing. I think

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.190
signs I've come to think represent a failure of
imagination both symbolically and literally in

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:45.820
how to change behavior.

00:30:45.820 --> 00:30:47.320
There's evidence that

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:50.970
context and environmental cues can be just as if
not more powerful.

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:52.490
Behavioral

00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:56.179
economist Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein have been 
arguing in their book "Nudge"

00:30:56.179 --> 00:30:59.990
that pushing people in the right direction through
what they call choice architecture can have powerful

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:01.469
effects, often more powerful than

00:31:01.469 --> 00:31:03.700
actually spelling out what people are supposed
to do.

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:05.659
They have a favorite example of this, which is
the

00:31:05.659 --> 00:31:09.480
urinals in the men's bathroom in Schipol Airport
in Amsterdam.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:11.660
This seems strange, but apparently the

00:31:11.660 --> 00:31:16.610
management was upset with having to do excessive
cleanups of men's careless behavior in the bathrooms of

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:18.570
the airport, so they painted sort of this kind of 

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:19.870
fly device

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:24.000
here, and within weeks there was a measurable
improvement in the cleanliness

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:26.270
of the bathrooms. Men were encouraged to

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:27.640
aim for this, so

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:30.220
no science was required, a very simple, subtle

00:31:30.220 --> 00:31:31.750
bit of

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:33.460
choice architecture here.

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:37.240
We think how does this apply to the world of traffic,
well there's been some interesting

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:41.160
things like Chicago's Lakeshore Drive, the curve
at Oak Street beach,

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:43.960
home to a crash hot spot for a number of years.

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.140
People tend to treat Lakeshore drive like kind of an

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:48.030
interstate highway even though

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:49.790
max speed is about thirty five miles an hour.

00:31:49.790 --> 00:31:53.829
So the first response was to install more signs,

00:31:53.829 --> 00:31:55.580
sort of warning there's this curve ahead.

00:31:55.580 --> 00:31:58.929
Then there were some larger signs, then larger
flashing signs.

00:31:58.929 --> 00:32:01.490
None of this was seeming to have an effect.

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:06.450
The final approach was to sort of paint these tranverse
parallel bars on the road that actually grow closer

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.400
to another as you get closer to the curve.

00:32:08.400 --> 00:32:12.300
The sensation you have driving over it is that
you're driving faster than you actually are, 

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:14.629
sort of increasing your sense of risk

00:32:14.629 --> 00:32:16.950
without actually increasing the risk.

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:21.270
It creates sort of a sense of confusion, you slow down.

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:22.530
Accident rate has 

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:25.879
according to the Chicago Department of Public Transportation
actually gone down thirty six percent.

00:32:25.879 --> 00:32:27.859
They've actually added some signage, which has made it

00:32:27.859 --> 00:32:29.159
go down even more,

00:32:29.159 --> 00:32:30.610
but the key point is just with this

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:32.630
innovative paint treatment they were able to

00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:36.420
affect a big sort of change in that.

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:41.000
It's not always about adding paint. Sometimes 
removing paint is actually

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:42.560
the answer. A street...

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:44.900
interesting study in England looked at two roads,

00:32:44.900 --> 00:32:46.930
trying to reduce speeds on one road.

00:32:46.930 --> 00:32:50.950
So they took two roads, a sample road and this road,
and actually removed the center line

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:51.750
markings.

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:53.210
And we've come to assume these have

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:54.799
a safety purpose

00:32:54.799 --> 00:32:58.480
to make things better for everyone, but they
found that by removing the white lines,

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.450
drivers actually drove further apart

00:33:00.450 --> 00:33:02.400
and slower than they did on the road

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:05.850
with white lines, which is exactly what
they were trying to do.

00:33:05.850 --> 00:33:09.299
So, just to conclude here about sort of 

00:33:09.299 --> 00:33:11.460
two thoughts

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:13.409
divided by centuries but sort of united,

00:33:13.409 --> 00:33:17.010
Sir Isaac Newton and Henry Barnes, former traffic
commissioner of New York City, which is 

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:20.850
there's a lot of technology and engineering in traffic
world, but

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:25.230
there's also a great importance on the human
factor, which is something that I've been trying to

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.200
stress in my work. And I'll

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:29.250
stop now, and hopefully we'll have a

00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:30.809
I'm sure a very productive conversation. Thank you.

